<h2 id="coding">1. Coding ≠ Programming</h2>

<p>
Coding is only the act of writing instructions in a programming language. Programming includes planning, problem-solving, testing, and maintaining software.
</p>

<h3>Planning</h3>
<p>You always start by defining what you're building and why.</p>

<h3>User Flow</h3>
<p>For example, in an online shop, users browse products, add to cart, and complete checkout in a structured flow.</p>

<h3>Design & Architecture</h3>
<p>Before coding, systems are designed using diagrams and wireframes to understand how everything connects.</p>

<div class="tip-box">
Programming is not typing code — it is solving problems step by step.
</div>

<h2 id="webdev">2. Building a Website Is More Than Writing Code</h2>

<p>
I used to think websites were just HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. In reality, deployment, SEO, forms, hosting, and analytics are equally important.
</p>

<p>
For example, adding a contact form, creating a sitemap, and setting up Google Search Console are essential steps for making a website visible and functional.
</p>

<h2 id="databases">3. Databases Matter More Than You Think</h2>

<p>
Almost every application depends on databases. Understanding how data is stored, queried, and optimized is critical in real projects.
</p>

<h2 id="algorithms">4. Algorithms Are About Thinking, Not Memorizing</h2>

<p>
Algorithms are not about memorizing formulas but about learning how to break down problems logically and efficiently.
</p>

<div class="warning-box">
A common mistake is trying to memorize solutions instead of understanding logic.
</div>

<h2 id="reality">5. Real-World Projects Matter More Than Theory</h2>

<p>
Theory is important, but real understanding comes from building projects, making mistakes, and fixing real problems.
</p>

<div class="conclusion">
    <h2>Conclusion</h2>
    <p>
        Applied Informatics is not just a technical field — it is a combination of logic, creativity, and real-world problem solving. The earlier you start building projects, the faster you grow.
    </p>
